    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Small faults in the Entrada Formation within the footwall of the Moab Fault share many of the same basic strctural elements observed in the primary fault zones. These small faults have experienced at least two phases of deformation, one phase characterized by the deformation banding process, and the second phase characterized by jointing with subsequent shear localized along the joints. These faults demonstrate that the products of earlier phases of deformation play a significant role in localizing the operation of subsequent deformation processes. As a result the distribution and geometry of new structural elements depend on the presence of older elements. These parameters define the fault \"architecture.\" The fault architecture in turn controls fault properties like fault and damage zone dimensions, and permeability structure. We postulate that the small faults observed at the study area in Courthouse Canyon reflect the early stages of fault zone development experienced by the larger faults defining the Moab fault proper. Therefore characterizing the evolution of the small faults in terms of the processes that created them provides a means for recognizing and understanding the evolution of the larger, more complex fault zones.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
